no more heroes 3 is set to be released  soon and since i've never played a no  more heroes game before i thought now  would be the perfect time to pull it out  of my ever expanding backlog and try it  out and what better place to start than  the very first entry in the series  simply titled no more heroes it was  released for the nintendo wii in 2007 in  japan and 2008 everywhere else you'll  notice that this gameplay is in crispy  hd because i'm playing the switchboard  but the question remains is no more  heroes any good and does it hold up in  2021 well in this video i plan to answer  that question as i check out the  original no more heroes i know a lot of  gamers out there don't have much  patience at least that's what bishop the  dude at the video store said  [Music]  i don't care about titles or power i  just want to be number one  am i ready  anytime  [Music]  for those who don't know no more heroes  is about a 27 year old man named travis  touchdown who loves wrestling in anime  and lives at the no more heroes motel in  the fictional santa destroy california  and all he wants to do is become the  number one ranked assassin in the  country travis is very crude very blunt  and very  horny okay how about this if i become  number one will you do it with me the  game also never takes itself too  seriously for example if you want to  save your game you have to use the  toilet to do so  [Music]  if the sound of all that repulses you  then i'm sorry it's really not going to  get any better from here that's just how  no more heroes is we've got plenty of  other videos here on gamespot and if  there was ever a place to ask you to go  check them out this would be it no more  heroes is the brainchild of one goichi  suda aka sudo51 if you're interested in  learning more about sudo51 we did an  episode of our show audio logs with him  where he broke down his iconic career a  link to that video will be in the  description here's the beam katana and  and this was not inspired by star wars  that's what i've been telling people for  years for safety no more heroes was  originally planned for the xbox 360  under the working title project heroes  but after the executive producer  suggested the then unknown wii console  suda saw the appeal and saw how the wii  remote would work after some initial  hesitation if you ask me the wii motion  controls and sword play go hand in hand  so this was a smart move to help the  game stand out as an m rated wii  exclusive  [Music]  anyways enough about the backstory how's  the game itself and should you go back  and play it in the lead up to three well  first let's start with the good aspects  of no more heroes travis wins a beam  katana totally not a lightsaber from an  online auction and with that he's able  to slice his way through anyone that  stands between him and his goal what's  unique about the combat is that after  taking down an enemy's health bar you're  instructed to swing the remote in a  direction shown on screen and then bam  you just slice them clean and are  treated to a lot of animated blood and a  very harrowing scream  after you successfully land this death  blow as the game calls it you'll see a  little slot machine appear at the bottom  of the screen and if you're lucky and  you get three in a row then you'll get a  special ability like strawberry on the  shortcake that makes your beam katana  highly charged increases your movement  speed and lets you do a death blow in  any direction after hitting an enemy  once strawberry on the shark tank  and as mentioned earlier not only is  travis a big anime loving otaku but he  also loves wrestling and if the  opportunity presents itself then you can  lay the smackdown on your enemies by  following some simple on-screen prompts  after stunning them and while all this  is fun the game's combat system doesn't  truly evolve throughout the course of  the game you can and will unlock new  beam katanas as you go on and you can  buy some upgrades for them but it's just  the same slicing and dicing over and  over until you're the number one ranked  assassin you know what i think the  combat is like though it's like eating a  bag of potato chips you know that  feeling when you've been munching on  chips for a while and even though you  might be done with it your mouth is just  craving more munching that's the best  way i could sum up the combat of this  game so even though it doesn't exactly  change in any significant way the combat  is still a lot of fun and seriously  addictive it is really cool how they all  die also i'd be remiss if i didn't  mention that charging up the beam katana  requires you to shake the remote as if  you're jacking off  if that isn't the perfect self-aware use  of the wii remote or joy-con then i  don't know what is however if you're  playing the nintendo switch port you do  get the benefit of using button controls  instead of motion controls if you choose  i like the button controls a lot but the  impact of swinging the beam katana  certainly gets lost although i do like  how the right stick is used to mimic the  sword actions taking a page out of  skyward sword hd's book or did skyward  sword mimic no more heroes the other  great aspect of this game is the ranked  assassin fights which is a good thing  because if those weren't fun then the  entire game would crumble the ranked  assassin fights are like boss battles  but they play a larger part in the story  too travis starts the game at rank  number 11 and to become number one he  has to take down each ranked assassin  one by one and climb the list who  manages this list you might ask the  united assassins association aka the uaa  why does the uaa exist play the game and  find out what works so well though is  that each ranked assassin not only has a  unique style and personality but each  fight keeps you on your toes and has  something different to throw at you  check it out  but at the same time each assassin  doesn't get a whole lot of screen time  you don't even get to know a whole lot  about them but all their stories are  kind of tragic and they're either filled  with regret or jaded by the job their  ego is so massive it has blinded them  you're no assassin you're just a  perverted killing maniac in essence  they're the same don't go on thinking  you're better than me the game  definitely doesn't glorify these people  and instead it shows the harsh realities  of achieving your dream and the baggage  that comes with it even when travis  himself is so blinded by being number  one  man this is what i live for fighting  your own kind nothing's more gratifying  i won't spoil anything major because if  you're interested so far i do encourage  you to try the game for yourself but  ultimately the ranked assassin battles  are a lot of fun to play and add a lot  of interesting layers to the overall  story  now you might be thinking to yourself  okay well some addictive combat that  feels like junk food and a bunch of  unique and interesting enemies probably  a short and fun game right well let's  get into the more divisive parts of this  game that don't make it ages gracefully  after beating one of the ranked  assassins you'll need to pay an entry  fee to fight the next one and that entry  fee goes up every time usually about 50  000 more lb dollars as they're called in  game there are two main things you could  do to earn the cash first you can go to  the job center as a new job is posted  after each ranked assassin fight usually  these jobs consist of mundane tasks like  collecting coconuts or mowing grass  if you score a silver medal or better at  these jobs you'll be recommended for  two new assassination gigs at k  entertainment a corrupt ad agency that  employs people to  assassinate for money usually after  doing the side job and assassination  gigs you'll have enough money to get to  the next ranked assassin battle but  there's also naomi who can upgrade your  beam katana and the thunder ryu building  where you can train to become stronger  and increase your health  [Music]  those things also cost money so if you  decide to spend your money there then  you might need to redo a side job or  assassination gig to get the amount  needed for the next ranked battle and on  top of that to even get from place to  place you need to ride your motorcycle  around the town of santa destroy and  honestly the town is so barren that all  you'll really be doing is going from  place to place over and over again i  guess it makes sense in a city where  assassins reigned supreme and tons of  henchmen openings exist that the town  lost all of its residents to murder but  my point is this all feels very  repetitive and mundane and it starts to  feel like a chore after a while now  here's the thing about all this it's  boring there's no getting around that  but it also feels  intentional by design like think about  it if you want to become number one in  anything you got to work hard to be  number one sometimes you have to work  odd jobs like collecting garbage or  filling cars with gas to ultimately  achieve your  goals they also say practice makes  perfect and doing these extra  assassination gigs helps travis improve  his combat skills even if for the player  it's just a video game in an interview  with endgamer suda51 said and i quote we  want to include the message in our games  that some jobs aren't important and you  may hate them but if you do them to the  best of your ability it'll turn out well  better times will come but this begs the  question just because this feels  intentional to the style and message of  the game do you have to put up with it  in a video game a place where you want  to escape the realities of the world now  ultimately that's on you to decide but  no matter how repetitive the game got i  constantly thought about how this all  felt intentional in the goal to become  number one and that kept me going the  game is somehow making fun of video game  tropes like the act of senseless  violence without remorse while also  pointing out that life isn't some  fantasy world where everything can  always work out exactly as you want  that's not a good thing you know  seeking meaning in everything  especially killing so now the answer  you've all been waiting for does the  original no more heroes hold up in 2021  to that i say yes yes it does i think it  can be hard to appreciate no more heroes  on a surface level and i can easily see  why someone would be turned off by the  many things i've mentioned so far but i  also think it's important to look at no  more heroes on its level and understand  where it's coming from this feels like a  very personal story from its creator one  that tells the tale of an otaku who gets  to live out his fantasy but also deal  with the harsh realities of living said  fantasy and despite how i feel about the  more repetitive nature of the game it  only took me 10 hours to beat so it's  really not that much of a grind plus i  barely even scratch the surface of what  happens in the plot of no more heroes  it's a wild ride especially as it goes  on and you're in for a treat if you  decide to play it it's also very helpful  that both no more heroes and no more  heroes 2 reported to the nintendo switch  and pc in the lead up to no more heroes  3 so it's easier than ever to play both  games and catch up on the series you  don't need to find a second hand copy of  the original wii version or play the ps3  port which many believe is a lesser  experience although i've never tried it  personally but if you're already looking  forward to no more heroes 3 then  consider subscribing to our channel as  we'll have a video review and maybe more  if you want it watching this video like  you just did and letting us know in the  comments that you want more no more  heroes content would definitely help  also if you like this format of  revisiting the original entry in a  series in the lead up to the latest  release then let me know that as well  this is a new format i'm trying and if  it does well then i'll consider doing  more and lastly if you did make it this  far and you're listening to me right now  thank you so much for watching i really  do appreciate it for all things gaming  keep it here on gamespot  [Music]  you

